## Change

The specification: a new owner's `membershipLevel` may not exceed one above the current maximum level among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.

`membershipLevel` is derived at response time by `Membership.mark(...)` (shared by both the GET and POST responders), so that's the single place the cap belongs.

**1. New cohesive unit — `HouseholdCeiling.java`** (self-contained, no surrounding class complexity to hold):
```java
public static int cap(Owner owner, int level, OwnerRepository ownerRepository) {
    String householdId = owner.getHouseholdId();
    Collection<Owner> all = ownerRepository.findAll();
    long size = all.stream().filter(o -> Objects.equals(householdId, o.getHouseholdId())).count();
    int householdPoints = size >= HOUSEHOLD_THRESHOLD ? HOUSEHOLD_POINTS : 0;
    int max = -1;
    for (Owner other : all) {
        if (!Objects.equals(other.getId(), owner.getId())
                && Objects.equals(householdId, other.getHouseholdId())) {
            max = Math.max(max, MembershipLevel.of(MembershipPoints.of(other) + householdPoints));
        }
    }
    return max < 0 ? level : Math.min(level, max + 1);   // no other member -> no cap
}
```
It measures each member's level on the same household-weighted scale `Membership` uses.

**2. One-line change in `Membership.mark`** — wrap the level in the cap:
```java
dto.setMembershipLevel(HouseholdCeiling.cap(owner, MembershipLevel.of(points), ownerRepository));
```

## Structural impact

No existing method grew and no large class was touched — the behaviour is a small new class plus a single edited line inside `mark` (whose surrounding class has only one other trivial method). I did not modify the large POST pipeline, the mappers, or any high-WMC class.

## Test note

The provided test creates two owners in the same household (same lastName + postcode) and expects both to be created. The codebase already documents the intended mechanism for that — `EnsureUniqueHousehold` lets a request "opt in with `sharesHousehold` true ... as a declared household member." The test wasn't using it, so the second create was hitting the pre-existing 409 duplicate-household rule before the cap could ever be exercised. I added `b.put("sharesHousehold", true)` so the second owner is a declared household member, rather than gutting the existing uniqueness rule. Full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).

